# Page load-status indicator (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-19):

> **Load status indicator on the top bar is a hard rule for all pages
> in the product.**
>
> **Expose it as a DOM attribute for tests, not just a spinner** —
> something like `data-app-busy="true|false"` on a stable root element.
> Tests wait on the attribute; operators see the indicator. One source
> of truth, no duplicate logic.
>
> **Make it three states, not two: busy, ready, and error.** If error
> collapses into ready, a page that failed its fetch will satisfy the
> wait and the crawl will move on having "verified" a broken page.

## The rule

> **Every operator page inherits a global top-bar load-status indicator
> derived from the shared data layer — never hand-wired per page. The
> same state drives a visible operator indicator and a stable DOM
> attribute for tests. There are three states: `busy`, `ready`, and
> `error`. An `error` state must never satisfy a readiness wait. Adding
> a new page must not require remembering to wire the indicator.**

## Sequencing (implementation gate)

This rule is **binding policy now**. Implementation of the indicator is
the improvement slice **after** the localization pass across all pages
completes and verifies. That pass is **every shipped pack derived from
US English (`en`)** — `en-GB`, `de`, `fr`, `es`, `zh-Hans`, `zh-TW`, and
any later tag — at equal weight. A single-tag walk is not the gate.
Until that slice lands, do not claim the attribute exists in the
product; do not rewrite nav-crawl waits onto a missing attribute. Once
implemented, the indicator binds **all** pages from that point —
including every page added afterward.

Do **not** treat this rule file as delivery of the indicator
([`shipped-means-served.mdc`](shipped-means-served.mdc)).

## When it applies

Any admin SPA (or other React operator shell) page that loads or mutates
data through the shared query / mutation client. Comprehensive UI /
nav-crawl / e2e claims that wait for a page to be "ready." New pages
and layout chrome that sit under the product shell.

Does **not** excuse skipping the indicator on "simple" pages, chrome-
only landings, or dialogs that own their own fetch — the shell still
reflects global fetch / mutation / error state.

## Required

1. **Three states, never two** — `busy` | `ready` | `error`. Map them
   onto a stable root attribute (for example
   `data-app-state="busy|ready|error"` and/or
   `data-app-busy="true|false"` where `true` means busy only — never
   treat `error` as not-busy / ready). Tests that wait for readiness
   MUST require `ready` explicitly and MUST fail or BLOCK on `error`.

2. **One source of truth** — derive globally from the data layer
   (React Query `useIsFetching()` / `useIsMutating()` plus query /
   mutation error state, or the equivalent shared client). **Not**
   per-page `useState(busy)` flags, **not** route-local spinners as the
   authority, **not** duplicated "is this page ready?" helpers that
   diverge from the indicator.

3. **Machine-readable + human-visible from the same state** — one
   derivation feeds both:
   - a DOM attribute on a **stable root** element (shell / AppBar
     container) that Playwright and nav-crawl can wait on;
   - a **visible top-bar** indicator operators can see.
   No parallel logic for the two consumers.

4. **Automatic inheritance** — mount once in the product shell (layout
   / AppBar). New pages inherit it without a checklist item to "add
   the busy flag."

5. **Accessibility** — the visible indicator uses `role="status"` with
   an appropriate `aria-live` region; meets Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA;
   never distinguishes the three states by color alone
   ([`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §2,
   [`operator-ui-credibility.mdc`](operator-ui-credibility.mdc)).

6. **Localized** — indicator text is catalog-driven for every shipped
   tag, not hardcoded English
   ([`compliance-artifacts-must-localize.mdc`](compliance-artifacts-must-localize.mdc)).
   New keys land with EN + every required Tier 1 tag in the same
   change set when the implementation slice ships.

7. **Honest failure** — a busy state that never clears is a product
   defect to surface, not to hide. A failed fetch shows `error`, never
   a silent `ready`
   ([`silent-failure-is-a-bug.mdc`](silent-failure-is-a-bug.mdc)).

8. **Tests consume the attribute** — once the attribute exists, e2e /
   nav-crawl waits on it (and on `ready` specifically). Heuristic
   waits are then forbidden for readiness (see Forbidden).

9. **Mirror** this rule to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same
   change set when editing the workspace copy
   ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).

## Forbidden

- Two-state busy/ready (or busy/idle) where **error collapses into
  ready** or into "not busy."
- Per-page hand-wired flags as the **source of truth** for global
  readiness (local dialog busy for a submit button is fine; claiming
  the page is ready from that flag is not).
- A test-only attribute with no operator-visible top-bar indicator, or
  a spinner / bar with no test-observable attribute.
- Duplicate logic: one code path for the DOM attribute and a different
  path for the visible chrome.
- Waiting on `networkidle`, fixed multi-second sleeps, or content-
  marker heuristics **once the attribute exists** — use the attribute
  ([`nav-crawl-console-coverage.mdc`](nav-crawl-console-coverage.mdc)).
- Claiming a page verified, crawled, or PASS when the readiness signal
  was `error` or never resolved to `ready`.
- Shipping the implementation before the gated localization pass has
  completed and verified, then back-filling locale keys later as an
  afterthought.

## Prior art (reuse-robert-core-first)

Surveyed read-only under `mix-server` / `crates/massman` and shared
`pim-ui-kit` (2026-08-19). **No reusable global page-load three-state
indicator exists.** Closest pieces:

| Location | What it is | Verdict |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Per-view `busy` / `CircularProgress` across `mix-server/ui/packages/pro` | Hand-wired dialog and page busy flags | **Partial pattern** — formalize and **supersede** for global readiness; keep local submit busy where appropriate |
| `pim-ui-kit` `ServerStatusIndicators` / `ServerConnectionBanner` | AppBar WebSocket / dispatch / zone connectivity | **Adjacent only** — not page fetch readiness; do not overload |
| `mix-server/ui/packages/app/.../SetupProgress.tsx` | Onboarding checklist card | **Unrelated** |
| `massman` MCP `ProgressSender` | Long-running job progress notifications | **Unrelated** (ops progress, not SPA page load) |
| `mix-server` test pacing (`networkidle` / `domcontentloaded`) | Heuristic waits | **Anti-pattern** this rule replaces once the attribute exists |

At implementation time: prefer a thin AIC shell component (React Query
hooks + AppBar mount). If extensive mix-server changes were required to
reuse Robert's tree, [`fork-locked-robert-repos.mdc`](fork-locked-robert-repos.mdc)
would apply — current survey does **not** justify a fork; there is
nothing to pin as-is.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation:** Nav crawl uses `networkidle` and a heading assert. A
page's list query fails; the route still paints chrome; the crawl
marks PASS. Separately, each new page adds its own `const [loading,
setLoading] = useState(true)` spinner that is forgotten on the next
page — tests have nothing stable to wait on.

**Fix (implementation slice, after localization verifies):** Mount one
`AppLoadStatus` (name flexible) in the shell AppBar. Derive
`busy` / `ready` / `error` from `useIsFetching`, `useIsMutating`, and
error state. Set `data-app-state` (and related busy attribute) on the
stable root from that value; render the same state as a top-bar
`role="status"` indicator with localized strings and non-color cues.
Nav-crawl waits for `data-app-state="ready"`; `error` fails the route.
No per-page wiring.

